UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 2, Lesson 2: Calming strategies: breathing and stretching', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Slow breathing and gentle stretching tell the body it is safe to relax. In belly breathing you breathe in slowly through the nose while the belly rises, then out slowly through the mouth, repeated several times. A gentle stretch is held without bouncing and stopped if anything hurts. Do these in a clear space and only at your own comfortable level; sitting versions are just as good.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA friend breathes in fast and loudly when upset. Write the steps you would tell them for belly breathing and for one safe stretch, including when to stop.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSteps: sit comfortably with a hand on the belly, breathe in slowly through the nose so the belly rises, out slowly through the mouth, repeat five times. Stretch: reach up tall and hold without bouncing. Stop if it hurts or you feel dizzy, then sit and breathe normally.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming. Calming breaths are slow and gentle, and stretches should feel comfortable, never painful.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nTeach one breathing strategy and one gentle stretch to a family member. Write the steps in numbered order, record their feeling number before and after, and add one adjustment for someone who cannot do the stretch standing up.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Slow breathing and gentle stretching tell the body it is safe to relax. In belly breathing you breathe in slowly through the nose while the belly rises, then out slowly through the mouth, repeated several times. A gentle stretch is held without bouncing and stopped if anything hurts. Do these in a clear space and only at your own comfortable level; sitting versions are just as good.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-SMI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Teach one breathing strategy and one gentle stretch to a family member. Write the steps in numbered order, record their feeling number before and after, and add one adjustment for someone who cannot do the stretch standi Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Example: five-finger breathing. Hold one hand up and trace it with a finger of the other hand. Breathe in slowly as you slide up a finger and out slowly as you slide down. After one hand, do three slow backwards shoulder rolls. Feeling rating before: 4 out of 5 frustrated. After: 2.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming. Calming breaths are slow and gentle, and stretches should feel comfortable, never painful. Correction: Steps: sit comfortably with a hand on the belly, breathe in slowly through the nose so the belly rises, out slowly through the mouth, repeat five times. Stretch: reach up tall and hold without bouncing. Stop if it hurts or you feel dizzy, then sit and breathe normally.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming. Calming breaths are slow and gentle, and stretches should feel comfortable, never painful. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Slow breathing and gentle stretching tell the body it is safe to relax. In belly breathing you breathe in slowly through the nose while the belly rises, then out slowly through the mouth, repeated several times. A gentle stretch is held without bouncing and stopped if anything hurts. Do these in a clear space and only at your own comfortable level; sitting versions are just as good. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Slow breathing and gentle stretching tell the body it is safe to relax. In belly breathing you breathe in slowly through the nose while the belly rises, then out slowly through the mouth, repeated several times. A gentle stretch is held without bouncing and stopped if anything hurts. Do these in a clear space and only at your own comfortable level; sitting versions are just as good.","Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming. Calming breaths are slow and gentle, and stretches should feel comfortable, never painful.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and demonstrate\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the reasoning\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming. Calming breaths are slow and gentle, and stretches should feel comfortable, never painful.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Leave someone out because the game felt easier that way."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation.","Leave someone out because the game felt easier that way.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new situation\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Steps: sit comfortably with a hand on the belly, breathe in slowly through the nose so the belly rises, out slowly through the mouth, repeat five times. Stretch: reach up tall and hold without bouncing. Stop if it hurts or you feel dizzy, then sit and breathe normally. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["Steps: sit comfortably with a hand on the belly, breathe in slowly through the nose so the belly rises, out slowly through the mouth, repeat five times. Stretch: reach up tall and hold without bouncing. Stop if it hurts or you feel dizzy, then sit and breathe normally.","Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming. Calming breaths are slow and gentle, and stretches should feel comfortable, never painful.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"A friend breathes in fast and loudly when upset. Write the steps you would tell \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\". Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["The most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"List the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Steps: sit comfortably with a hand on the belly, breathe in slowly through the nose so the belly rises, out slowly through the mouth, repeat five times. Stretch: reach up tall and hold without bouncing. Stop if it hurts or you feel dizzy, then sit and breathe normally. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["Steps: sit comfortably with a hand on the belly, breathe in slowly through the nose so the belly rises, out slowly through the mouth, repeat five times. Stretch: reach up tall and hold without bouncing. Stop if it hurts or you feel dizzy, then sit and breathe normally.","Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming. Calming breaths are slow and gentle, and stretches should feel comfortable, never painful.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["The new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a sh\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant evidence with accurate reasoning and named cues, rules or choices.","question":"Describe or practise a fresh situation that uses the central skill. Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: Example: five-finger breathing. Hold one hand up and trace it with a finger of the other hand. Breathe in slowly as you slide up a finger and out slowly as you slide down. After one hand, do three slow backwards shoulder rolls. Feeling rating before: 4 out of 5 frustrated. After: 2.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change the choice or strategy, not just make the answer longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus a corrected choice and a reason linked to safety, health or respect.","question":"Name one likely misconception or unsafe choice for this topic, correct it and say why the correction is safer, healthier or more respectful.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking a stretch should hurt to work, or that breathing fast and deep is calming. Calming breaths are slow and gentle, and stretches should feel comfortable, never painful. Correction: Steps: sit comfortably with a hand on the belly, breathe in slowly through the nose so the belly rises, out slowly through the mouth, repeat five times. Stretch: reach up tall and hold without bouncing. Stop if it hurts or you feel dizzy, then sit and breathe normally.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w02-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise belly breathing.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Health, Wellbeing and Relationships","success_criteria":["I can use belly breathing with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a gentle stretch and explain why it matters.","I can practise slow and steady independently on a new prompt.","I can notice stopping safely needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Slow breathing and gentle stretching tell the body it is safe to relax. In belly breathing you breathe in slowly through the nose while the belly rises, then out slowly through the mouth, repeated several times. A 