table, never painful. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSlow breathing and gentle stretching tell the body it is safe to relax. In belly breathing you breathe in slowly through the nose while the belly rises, then out slowly through the mouth, repeated several times. A gentle stretch is held without bouncing and stopped if anything hurts. Do these in a clear space and only at your own comfortable level; sitting versions are just as good. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be 